{"duration_api_ms":33555,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"187d727c-8fc0-403d-beec-e4a6e85bbe5a","total_cost_usd":0.25531875000000004,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3099,"cache_read_input_tokens":2075,"output_tokens":3856,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3099,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3856,"cache_read_input_tokens":2075,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3099,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3099},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-fable-5-1":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":3856,"cacheReadInputTokens":2075,"cacheCreationInputTokens":3099,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.25531875000000004,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-fable-5-1","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"claims\":[{\"text\":\"The document \\\"Nimbrel Ledger append rejected with HTTP 429\\\" was authored by Priya Raghunathan.\",\"line\":3,\"span\":\"Author: Priya Raghunathan\"},{\"text\":\"The document \\\"Nimbrel Ledger append rejected with HTTP 429\\\" was updated on 2026-03-18.\",\"line\":4,\"span\":\"Updated: 2026-03-18\"},{\"text\":\"Append requests to Nimbrel Ledger come back with HTTP 429 `Too Many Requests`.\",\"line\":8,\"span\":\"Append requests to Nimbrel Ledger come back with HTTP 429 `Too Many Requests`.\"},{\"text\":\"When Nimbrel Ledger append requests are refused with HTTP 429, Nimbrel Relay stalls.\",\"line\":8,\"span\":\"Nimbrel Relay stalls\"},{\"text\":\"When Nimbrel Ledger append requests are refused with HTTP 429, batch loaders resend the same envelope.\",\"line\":8,\"span\":\"batch loaders resend the same envelope\"},{\"text\":\"When Nimbrel Ledger append requests are refused with HTTP 429, reads against the same shard begin to time out.\",\"line\":8,\"span\":\"reads against the same shard begin to time out\"},{\"text\":\"The HTTP 429 append refusal from Nimbrel Ledger reaches the client.\",\"line\":8,\"span\":\"The refusal reaches the client and is written to the Relay and Collector logs as well.\"},{\"text\":\"The HTTP 429 append refusal from Nimbrel Ledger is written to the Relay and Collector logs.\",\"line\":8,\"span\":\"The refusal reaches the client and is written to the Relay and Collector logs as well.\"},{\"text\":\"In the example refusal envelope, the append batch was refused after 0 of 64 envelopes.\",\"line\":13,\"span\":\"append batch refused after 0 of 64 envelopes\"},{\"text\":\"In the example refusal envelope, the error type is nbl_queue_refused_exception.\",\"line\":13,\"span\":\"\\\"type\\\":\\\"nbl_queue_refused_exception\\\"\"},{\"text\":\"In the example refusal envelope, the reason given is \\\"refused append on ingest path\\\".\",\"line\":13,\"span\":\"refused append on ingest path\"},{\"text\":\"In the example refusal envelope, ingest_and_leader_bytes is 88121344.\",\"line\":13,\"span\":\"ingest_and_leader_bytes=88121344\"},{\"text\":\"In the example refusal envelope, follower_bytes is 212992.\",\"line\":13,\"span\":\"follower_bytes=212992\"},{\"text\":\"In the example refusal envelope, all_bytes is 88334336.\",\"line\":13,\"span\":\"all_bytes=88334336\"},{\"text\":\"In the example refusal envelope, ingest_op_bytes is 155648.\",\"line\":13,\"span\":\"ingest_op_bytes=155648\"},{\"text\":\"In the example refusal envelope, max_ingest_bytes is 88080384.\",\"line\":13,\"span\":\"max_ingest_bytes=88080384\"},{\"text\":\"In the example refusal envelope, the status is 429.\",\"line\":13,\"span\":\"\\\"status\\\":429\"},{\"text\":\"The Ledger operations handbook covers refused appends under Refused Appends.\",\"line\":16,\"span\":\"The Ledger operations handbook now covers the same ground under Refused Appends, with a walkthrough for each of the three refusal paths set out below.\"},{\"text\":\"The Ledger operations handbook has a walkthrough for each of the three refusal paths.\",\"line\":16,\"span\":\"The Ledger operations handbook now covers the same ground under Refused Appends, with a walkthrough for each of the three refusal paths set out below.\"},{\"text\":\"Every Nimbrel Ledger release on every platform can refuse an append with HTTP 429.\",\"line\":20,\"span\":\"Every Nimbrel Ledger release on every platform can refuse an append this way.\"},{\"text\":\"A `429 - Too Many Requests` reply is what a Ledger node sends once an append queue or a read queue is full.\",\"line\":24,\"span\":\"A [`429 - Too Many Requests` reply](https://docs.nimbrel.example/ledger/http-status-codes) is what a Ledger node sends once it has nowhere left to put the work, which is to say once [an append queue or a read queue is full](https://docs.nimbrel.example/ledger/why-appends-are-refused).\"},{\"text\":\"There are 3 ways a Nimbrel Ledger append draws a 429.\",\"line\":26,\"span\":\"There are 3 ways an append draws a 429:\"},{\"text\":\"A Nimbrel Ledger append draws a 429 when the `append` or `system_append` worker pools hold more batches than they have slots for.\",\"line\":28,\"span\":\"The `append` or `system_append` worker pools hold more batches than they have slots for (`nbctl pool status --all`)\"},{\"text\":\"The command `nbctl pool status --all` shows the state of the `append` and `system_append` worker pools.\",\"line\":28,\"span\":\"The `append` or `system_append` worker pools hold more batches than they have slots for (`nbctl pool status --all`)\"},{\"text\":\"A Nimbrel Ledger append draws a 429 when the ingest memory guard has refused the batch.\",\"line\":29,\"span\":\"The ingest memory guard has refused the batch (`nbctl guard report ingest --counters`).\"},{\"text\":\"The command `nbctl guard report ingest --counters` reports ingest memory guard refusals.\",\"line\":29,\"span\":\"The ingest memory guard has refused the batch (`nbctl guard report ingest --counters`).\"},{\"text\":\"A Nimbrel Ledger append draws a 429 when a circuit breaker has tripped.\",\"line\":30,\"span\":\"A circuit breaker has tripped (`nbctl breaker list --tripped`)\"},{\"text\":\"The command `nbctl breaker list --tripped` lists tripped circuit breakers.\",\"line\":30,\"span\":\"A circuit breaker has tripped (`nbctl breaker list --tripped`)\"},{\"text\":\"A Nimbrel Ledger circuit breaker trips on any operation and is not particular to appends.\",\"line\":30,\"span\":\"a breaker trips on any operation and is not particular to appends\"},{\"text\":\"From release 4.6 and release 5.1 onward, appending to a `wide_text` column draws a 429 on its own account whenever the batch would otherwise have run the node out of memory.\",\"line\":32,\"span\":\"[1] **From release 4.6 and release 5.1 onward**, appending to a `wide_text` column draws a 429 on its own account whenever the batch would otherwise have run the node out of memory.\"},{\"text\":\"The workaround for Nimbrel Ledger 429 append refusals is to take append and query load off the cluster for long enough that the queues drain and the nodes fall back under their limits.\",\"line\":36,\"span\":\"Take append and query load off the cluster for long enough that the queues drain and the nodes fall back under their limits.\"},{\"text\":\"Where batches carry `wide_text` columns and the cluster runs release 4.6 or release 5.1 or later, the workaround is to send fewer envelopes per batch.\",\"line\":38,\"span\":\"Where batches carry `wide_text` columns and the cluster runs release 4.6 or release 5.1 or later, send fewer envelopes per batch.\"},{\"text\":\"A Nimbrel Ledger cluster that refuses appends has been given more work than its hardware can carry.\",\"line\":42,\"span\":\"A cluster that refuses appends has been given more work than its hardware can carry, so the answer is hardware: larger nodes (scale up), or more of them (scale out).\"},{\"text\":\"The resolution for a Nimbrel Ledger cluster that refuses appends is larger nodes (scale up) or more nodes (scale out).\",\"line\":42,\"span\":\"A cluster that refuses appends has been given more work than its hardware can carry, so the answer is hardware: larger nodes (scale up), or more of them (scale out).\"},{\"text\":\"Per the sizing notes, scale up first in most cases.\",\"line\":42,\"span\":\"Scale up first in most cases, and scale out when what is wanted is a further copy of the data for availability, per [the sizing notes](https://docs.nimbrel.example/ledger/sizing).\"},{\"text\":\"Per the sizing notes, scale out when what is wanted is a further copy of the data for availability.\",\"line\":42,\"span\":\"Scale up first in most cases, and scale out when what is wanted is a further copy of the data for availability, per [the sizing notes](https://docs.nimbrel.example/ledger/sizing).\"},{\"text\":\"Splitting a hot stream over more leader shards spreads append load across more nodes.\",\"line\":44,\"span\":\"Splitting a hot stream over more leader shards spreads append load across more nodes, which helps in some layouts.\"},{\"text\":\"For `wide_text` columns on release 4.6 and release 5.1 and later, the first step is cutting the number of envelopes in each append batch.\",\"line\":48,\"span\":\"1. Begin by cutting the number of envelopes in each append batch.\"},{\"text\":\"For `wide_text` columns on release 4.6 and release 5.1 and later, where smaller batches do not clear the refusals, the second step is to add processor and memory capacity to the nodes.\",\"line\":49,\"span\":\"2. Where smaller batches do not clear it, add processor and memory capacity to the nodes.\"},{\"text\":\"For `wide_text` columns on release 4.6 and release 5.1 and later, raising the `ingest_guard.memory.leader.ceiling` cluster setting is the third step, taken only after cutting batch size and adding capacity.\",\"line\":50,\"span\":\"3. Only then raise the `ingest_guard.memory.leader.ceiling` cluster setting, which defaults to 10% of the heap.\"},{\"text\":\"The `ingest_guard.memory.leader.ceiling` cluster setting defaults to 10% of the heap.\",\"line\":50,\"span\":\"3. Only then raise the `ingest_guard.memory.leader.ceiling` cluster setting, which defaults to 10% of the heap.\"},{\"text\":\"A higher `ingest_guard.memory.leader.ceiling` lets a node hold more in-flight append memory before refusing.\",\"line\":50,\"span\":\"A higher ceiling lets a node hold more in-flight append memory before refusing, and a node holding too much runs out of memory instead of refusing, which is the worse outcome.\"},{\"text\":\"A node holding too much in-flight append memory runs out of memory instead of refusing.\",\"line\":50,\"span\":\"A higher ceiling lets a node hold more in-flight append memory before refusing, and a node holding too much runs out of memory instead of refusing, which is the worse outcome.\"},{\"text\":\"The `ingest_guard.memory.leader.ceiling` setting is read at startup.\",\"line\":50,\"span\":\"The setting is read at startup, so the cluster must be restarted for a change to take.\"},{\"text\":\"The cluster must be restarted for a change to the `ingest_guard.memory.leader.ceiling` setting to take.\",\"line\":50,\"span\":\"The setting is read at startup, so the cluster must be restarted for a change to take.\"},{\"text\":\"Where hardware and setting changes are not available, the load itself must change: fewer appends, cheaper queries, or both.\",\"line\":52,\"span\":\"Where none of that is open to you, the load itself is what changes: fewer appends, cheaper queries, or both.\"},{\"text\":\"Refusals that show up only during a spike usually clear on their own once the queues drain.\",\"line\":52,\"span\":\"Refusals that show up only during a spike usually clear on their own once the queues drain.\"}]}","ttft_ms":33585,"type":"result","duration_ms":33624,"uuid":"724ac33c-f2a8-4568-8fb9-c1ca0a3837f3","ttft_stream_ms":2417,"time_to_request_ms":112,"first_content_frame_ms":2418,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
